# Model separation and data flow

Typical flow: **HTTP request** → (optional) **API request** + mapper → **MediatR command/query** → **handler** → **repository / entity** → **DTO** → JSON response.

| Type | Location | Purpose |
|------|----------|---------|
| **Entity** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.Data/Models/*.cs` | EF Core persistence model |
| **DTO** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.Business/Dtos/*.cs` | Shapes returned to the API / used between handlers |
| **Command / Query** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.Business/Cqrs/**/Commands|Queries/*.cs` | MediatR messages |
| **Handler** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.Business/Cqrs/**/Handlers/*.cs` | Orchestration, validation, mapping entity ↔ DTO |
| **API request** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.API/Requests/*.cs` | Incoming payloads when not bound as primitives only |
| **Mappers** | `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.API/Mappers/`, `backend/MotoklubBezbednost.Business/Mappings/` | Request ↔ command/query; entity ↔ DTO |

- Keep **entities** out of API contracts; expose **DTOs** (or dedicated response types) from controllers.
- When introducing **AutoMapper** or shared profiles, keep the same boundaries: API mapping vs Business mapping.
